trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The liver enzyme pattern is highly informative: markedly elevated ALP (2007 U/L) and GGT (154 U/L) — both cholestatic markers — confirm biliary obstruction or severe intrahepatic cholestasis. Mild-moderate AST/ALT elevation suggests associated hepatocellular injury. This profile strongly favors extrahepatic obstruction (e.g., biliary atresia) or genetic cholestatic syndromes (e.g., Alagille, PFIC). Since ultrasound was not yet reported, it remains the most critical next step — its findings (e.g., absent gallbladder, triangular cord sign) are diagnostic for biliary atresia. I must request it again, explicitly, as it’s time-sensitive (Kasai portoenterostomy is most effective if performed before 60 days of life).

[TEST] Abdominal ultrasound with specific evaluation for:  
- Presence, size, and contractility of the gallbladder;  
- Visualization of the common bile duct and intrahepatic ducts;  
- Triangular cord sign;  
- Hepatic echotexture and spleen size.

